Thinking: We need to answer user question: "How do I load a .pmtiles file as a source in MapLibre GL JS?" Provide step-by-step instructions, code example, mention installing pmtiles library, registering protocol, using url, handling CORS, hosting, etc. Use skill content. Provide explanation.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **single `.pmtiles` file** (vector, raster, or raster‑DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles protocol library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` URL scheme, so you need the tiny helper library that turns a range‑request to a static file into the TileJSON MapLibre expects.

```bash
# Using npm (or yarn/pnpm)
npm install pmtiles
```

> **Tip:** The library is only ~10 KB, has no native dependencies, and works in the browser as an ES‑module.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** before any map is created

Do this at the top‑level of your app (e.g. in `index.js`, a React context provider, or a plain script tag).  
If you register it inside a component that mounts/unmounts repeatedly you’ll end up with duplicate handlers.

```js
// index.js (or the first script that runs)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol handler
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Tell MapLibre to use it for the "pmtiles" scheme
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// OPTIONAL – clean‑up when the page is unloaded (helps with hot‑module‑reload)
window.addEventListener('unload', () => {
  maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
});
```

> **Why `pmtiles://`?**  
> The handler works with any URL that starts with `pmtiles://`. The part after the scheme can be a full HTTPS URL (or a relative path) that points at the `.pmtiles` file.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the file

### 3‑a. Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // ONE source that points at the whole archive
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // NOTE: use **url**, not **tiles**, so the protocol can read the header
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        // If the file lives next to your page you could also use:
        // url: 'pmtiles://./data/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that exists inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads from a different source‑layer in the same file
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1.5
        }
      }

      // …add as many layers as you need, all sharing the same source
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### 3‑b. Raster imagery

```js
// Inside the same `style` object, add a raster source
sources: {
  rasterPmtiles: {
    type: 'raster',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/ortho.pmtiles',
    tileSize: 256               // optional, defaults to 256
  }
},
layers: [
  {
    id: 'ortho',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'rasterPmtiles',
    paint: {}
  }
]
```

### 3‑c. Raster‑DEM (terrain)

```js
sources: {
  terrainPmtiles: {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/terrain/elevation.pmtiles',
    encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox" if that encoding was used
  }
},
terrain: {
  source: 'terrainPmtiles',
  exaggeration: 1.5
}
```

> **Important:**  
> *Always use the `url:` property.*  
> When you use `url:` the PMTiles protocol reads the archive header, extracts the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and returns a proper TileJSON to MapLibre. If you instead write a `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` template you lose that information and MapLibre will assume a default `maxzoom: 22`, causing unnecessary empty‑tile requests.

---

## 4️⃣ Host the `.pmtiles` file where it can be fetched with **HTTP Range** support

| Provider | How to enable range requests | CORS |
|----------|-----------------------------|------|
| **AWS S3** | Native support. Set the object to public or use signed URLs. | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) on the bucket CORS configuration. |
| **Cloudflare R2** | S3‑compatible, supports ranges out‑of‑the‑box. | Same CORS header as S3. |
| **GitHub Pages** | Works for files ≤ 100 MiB (the limit for a single file on a repo). | GitHub Pages sends `*` CORS automatically for public assets. |
| **Netlify / Vercel / Cloudflare Pages** | All serve static files with range support. | Add a `_headers` file (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`). |
| **Any CDN** | Ensure the origin returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. | Propagate the CORS header through the CDN. |

**Cache‑control tip:** Because the archive never changes (or you version it), set a long max‑age:

```
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
```

That lets browsers and CDNs cache the whole file (and each byte‑range response) for a year.

