## TL;DR  
MapLibre GL JS v4 changed the **protocol‑handler API**.  
The old `addProtocol(name, (url, callback) => …)` signature that called a
callback with `(err, data, headers)` no longer works.  

In v4 you must register a **function that returns a Promise (or an async
function)** which resolves to an object that contains the tile bytes
(`data`) and, optionally, HTTP‑like headers (`cacheControl`, `expires`,
`etag`). The function also receives an **AbortSignal** so you can cancel a
fetch when the map discards the request.

Below is a step‑by‑step migration guide, a minimal example of a custom
vector‑tile protocol, and a quick note on using the official **pmtiles**
handler (which already follows the new API).

---

## 1. What changed in v4?

| v3 (removed) | v4 (new) |
|--------------|----------|
| `maplibregl.addProtocol(name, (url, callback) => { … })` | `maplibregl.addProtocol(name, (params, abort) => { … })` |
| `callback(err, data, headers?)` | **return** a `Promise` (or `async` function) that resolves to `{ data, cacheControl?, expires?, etag? }` |
| No abort handling | `abort` is an `AbortController` – you should pass `abort.signal` to any `fetch`/XHR so the request can be cancelled. |
| The callback received only the raw URL string. | `params` is an object: `{ url, tile, zoom, x, y, ... }`. `url` is the full URL after the protocol prefix (e.g. `https://example.com/tiles/12/3456/7890.pbf`). |
| Tile‑request headers were ignored. | You can now supply `cacheControl`, `expires`, `etag` – MapLibre will forward them to the browser cache. |

Because the old callback is never called, the map never receives tile data,
so you see blank tiles or “Failed to load source” errors after the upgrade.

---

## 2. Minimal migration – from callback to async return

### v3 code (doesn’t work in v4)

```js
// v3 – removed in v4
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (url, callback) => {
  fetch(url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, new Uint8Array(buf), { 'cache-control': 'max-age=3600' }))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

### v4 replacement (async function)

```js
// v4 – new API
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abort) => {
  const { url } = params;               // full URL after the protocol prefix
  const response = await fetch(url, { signal: abort.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing an error will cause MapLibre to treat the tile as missing.
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
  }

  // The whole file is read into an ArrayBuffer; you can also stream if you
  // need progressive decoding.
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // Optional: expose cache‑related headers so the browser can cache the
  // range request correctly.
  const cacheControl = response.headers.get('cache-control');
  const expires = response.headers.get('expires');
  const etag = response.headers.get('etag');

  return {
    data: new Uint8Array(arrayBuffer),   // <-- required
    cacheControl,                       // optional
    expires,
    etag
  };
});
```

**Key points**

* **Return** an object, **don’t call a callback**.  
* The object **must** contain a `data` property that is a `Uint8Array` (or
  `ArrayBuffer`).  
* If you need to abort the request (e.g., the user pans away before the
  fetch finishes), pass `abort.signal` to `fetch`.  
* Any error you throw (or a rejected promise) tells MapLibre the tile is
  unavailable – it will be rendered as “no data”.

---

## 3. Full example – custom vector‑tile source

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 – custom protocol</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Register the protocol (once, before any map is created)
// ---------------------------------------------------
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async ({url}, abort) => {
  // Example: the URL after the protocol is a normal HTTP endpoint.
  // You could also rewrite it, add auth tokens, etc.
  const response = await fetch(url, {signal: abort.signal});

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const buf = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // Forward cache headers (optional but recommended)
  return {
    data: new Uint8Array(buf),
    cacheControl: response.headers.get('cache-control'),
    expires: response.headers.get('expires'),
    etag: response.headers.get('etag')
  };
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Create the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
      // Note the `url:` form – not `tiles:` – so MapLibre can read the
      // TileJSON (minzoom/maxzoom) from the archive if you ever switch
      // to a PMTiles file.
      mySource: {
        type: 'vector',
        // You can embed any templated URL here; the protocol handler will
        // receive the fully‑expanded URL.
        url: 'mytiles://https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'mySource',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // …more layers…
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why we use `url:` instead of `tiles:`  

When the source is a **PMTiles** archive (or any source that can provide a
TileJSON), MapLibre reads the archive header to discover `minzoom`,
`maxzoom`, bounds, etc. Using `url:` lets the protocol handler return a
*TileJSON* object (the same shape you would get from a normal `*.json`
endpoint). If you use the older `tiles: ['mytiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` form,
MapLibre assumes a default `maxzoom: 22` and will keep requesting zoom levels
that the archive does not contain, resulting in empty tiles.

---

## 4. Using the **official PMTiles** handler (no custom code needed)

If you are serving a **.pmtiles** file (vector, raster, or raster‑dem) you
don’t need to write your own protocol at all – the `pmtiles` NPM package
already implements the v4 API.

```bash
npm install pmtiles
```

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register the handler once (e.g. in your app entry point)
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

Now you can reference the archive like:

```json
{
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles"
}
```

The same handler works for raster (`type: "raster"`) and raster‑dem
(`type: "raster-dem", "encoding": "terrarium"`).

> **Tip:** If you already have a custom handler that does extra auth or
> request‑rewriting, you can *wrap* the pmtiles handler:

```js
const pm = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', async (params, abort) => {
  // Example: add a bearer token to every request
  const token = await getMyAuthToken();
  const url = new URL(params.url);
  url.searchParams.set('access_token', token);
  // Call the original pmtiles handler with the modified URL
  return pm.tile({ url: url.toString() }, abort);
});
```

---

## 5. Migration checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| **1** | Replace every `addProtocol(name, (url, cb) => …)` with `addProtocol(name, async (params, abort) => …)` (or a function that returns a Promise). |
| **2** | Inside the handler, **return** an object `{ data: Uint8Array, cacheControl?, expires?, etag? }`. Do **not** call a callback. |
| **3** | Pass `abort.signal` to any `fetch`/XHR so the request can be cancelled. |
| **4** | If you were using `tiles: [...]` in the style, switch to `url:` (or keep `tiles:` but be aware you lose automatic min/max‑zoom detection). |
| **5** | Ensure the host serving the tiles returns `Accept‑Ranges: bytes` and proper CORS (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*`). |
| **6** | If you are using PMTiles, simply install the `pmtiles` package and register its protocol – no custom code needed. |
| **7** | Test with the browser dev‑tools → Network → “Range” requests to verify the client is sending `Range: bytes=…` and receiving `206 Partial Content`. |
| **8** | (Optional) Add cache headers (`Cache‑Control: public, max‑age=31536000`) on the static file so CDNs cache the whole archive and range responses. |

---

## 6. Common pitfalls after the upgrade

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **All tiles are gray / missing** | Handler still uses the old callback signature → MapLibre never receives data. | Update to async return as shown above. |
| **`Failed to load source`** in console | The handler throws an error *or* returns an object without `data`. | Ensure you always return `{ data: Uint8Array }` on success. |
| **Range requests not sent** | Host does not advertise `Accept‑Ranges` or the URL is a data‑URI. | Serve the file from a static host that supports byte ranges (S3, Cloudflare R2, etc.). |
| **Tiles load but over‑zoom shows blanks** | Using `tiles:` instead of `url:` for a PMTiles source, so MapLibre assumes `maxzoom:22`. | Switch to `url:` (or provide explicit `maxzoom` in the source definition). |
| **CORS errors** | The bucket or CDN does not send `Access‑Control-Allow-Origin`. | Add `*` or your domain to the CORS config of the bucket/CDN. |
| **Abort errors in console** | You forgot to forward `abort.signal` to `fetch
